-- DICT-SEMANTICS §5, PHANTOM-POSITION case.
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived): §5 lists three cases for where the class parameter
-- `ā_C` may occur in a method type -- argument, result, and "PHANTOM POSITION
-- (`ā_C` absent from `τ_m`): SAME -- only the static dictionary determines it."
-- Read literally, the spec licenses a phantom method: `phantom : Int` on
-- `interface Mk a` is a legal class method whose dispatch comes from the
-- statically-determined dictionary, as `(method)` in §5 states with no
-- occurrence side condition. Under that reading this program is ACCEPTED and
-- prints 7.
--
-- OBSERVED -- A DIVERGENCE, ⚠️ UNFILED (no open issue found; reported to the
-- orchestrator with this repro on 2026-07-30 for triage):
--   check/run/build all REJECT at the IMPL site with a dedicated diagnostic code
--   `T-PHANTOM-METHOD`: "Method 'phantom' in interface 'Mk' does not mention
--   interface parameter(s) 'a'; cannot dispatch".
--
-- The dedicated code (`compiler/types/typecheck.mdk`'s `checkPhantomMethods`)
-- says this is a deliberate design decision, not an accident -- and it is
-- defensible: Medaka has no visible type application, so a use site of `phantom`
-- has no way to FIX `a`, and §5 never says how it would. What is missing is the
-- reconciliation: §5 still lists phantom position as supported, and nothing in
-- the tree records the decision. Either §5 should carve phantom position out
-- (naming the no-type-application reason) or the checker should allow it.
--
-- SEVERITY as observed: S3 (friction & debt). It is a LOUD, LOCATED reject with
-- a helpful message, and it is an OVER-rejection -- nothing unsound is accepted.
-- This row pins the reject, so it self-drains if the checker is ever relaxed.
interface Mk a where
  mk : Int -> a
  phantom : Int

data Red = Red Int

impl Mk Red where
  mk n = Red n
  phantom = 7

main = println (phantom : Int)
